%description:

RFC 9293 section 3.8.6.2.1, rule (4) — the override timeout of the sender SWS avoidance expires while the peer sends data.

The rule: "Send data: [...] (4) if data is PUSHed and the override timeout occurs." The override timeout forces the
transmission of data that the sender SWS avoidance holds (MUST-38 asks for the algorithm). Silly window avoidance is
out of the scope of the catalog of RFC 9293 (doc/project/evidence/standard/rfc9293/catalog.md, "Out of scope in this
catalog"), so this test checks the section directly. The reviewer document is ../sws-override-timer.md.

Host under test: host1, with an MSS of 1000 bytes and an RTO of 1 s, sends 5000 bytes. The TS option takes 12 bytes of
each segment, so a full segment holds 988 bytes. Peer: host2, with an advertised window of 3464 bytes, reads nothing
and sends 100 bytes to host1 at 0.35 s, 0.65 s and 0.95 s. After three segments, 2964 bytes, the window of host2 is
500 bytes: less than one segment, less than the queued data and less than half of the largest window, so host1 holds
the rest from about 0.2 s. Each segment of host2 acknowledges SND.UNA with the same window: it updates no window.

Observation: host1 sends the held data one RTO after the hold, at about 1.2 s, between the segments of host2 and
before 1.3 s. The fault BD-21 of plan/pending/baseline-catch-up-defects.md started the timer again at each segment of
host2, so the held data left only one RTO after the last segment of host2, at about 1.95 s.

Expected result: PASS.

%includes:
#include "ProtocolTest.h"
#include "TcpTestApp.h"

%global:

using namespace inet;
using namespace inet::protocoltest;

static void sendHundredBytes(TcpTestApp& app)
{
    app.sendData(app.accepted(0), 100);
}

Define_ProtocolTestProgram()
{
    return ProtocolTest("Rfc9293SwsOverridePeerData")
        .act(action().at(0.0).run<TcpTestApp>("host2.app[0]", [](TcpTestApp& app) {
            app.socket().setAutoRead(false);
            app.socket().bind(1000);
            app.socket().listen();
        }))
        .once(on("host2.tcp").signal("packetSentToLower")
             .filterPacket("tcp.ackBit == true && tcp.window == 500")
             .describe("the ACK of host2 that leaves a window of 500 bytes: host1 holds its data")
             .within(1.0))
        .meanwhile(never(on("host1.tcp").signal("packetSentToLower")
                             .filterPacket("tcp.synBit == false && pk.dataLength > tcp.headerLength")
                             .describe("held data before the override timeout, while host2 sends")
                             .within(0.9)))
        .act(action().at(0.35).run<TcpTestApp>("host2.app[0]", sendHundredBytes))
        .act(action().at(0.65).run<TcpTestApp>("host2.app[0]", sendHundredBytes))
        .act(action().at(0.95).run<TcpTestApp>("host2.app[0]", sendHundredBytes))
        .once(on("host1.tcp").signal("packetSentToLower")
             .filterPacket("tcp.synBit == false && pk.dataLength > tcp.headerLength")
             .describe("the held data, one RTO after the hold and before one RTO after the last segment of host2")
             .within(0.35));
}

%activity:
EV << "Rfc9293SwsOverridePeerData compiled\n";

%inifile: omnetpp.ini

[General]
**.tcp.mss = 1000
*.host2.tcp.advertisedWindow = 3464
*.host1.app[0].sendBytes = 5000B
*.host2.app[0].typename = "TcpTestApp"
include ../../parameter/ini/_bulk.ini
include ../../parameter/ini/_base.ini

%contains: stdout
PROTOCOLTEST Rfc9293SwsOverridePeerData: PASS
